The difference between finding luck and cultivating it.

If you pick every four-leaf clover you encounter, you collect the rarity but never give it the chance to become commonplace.

Biologically, picking a leaf doesn’t necessarily prevent the plant from reproducing. But your larger principle holds.

Extraction captures value. Stewardship compounds it.

And there’s another layer: the bee doesn’t need to possess the flower to benefit from it. In gathering nectar, it can help the flower reproduce.

Mutualism rather than mere consumption.

Now apply that to opportunities, relationships, knowledge, wealth, or even the Pantheon.

Sometimes the optimal move isn’t to capture an advantage. It’s to preserve the conditions that generate advantages.

And perhaps that’s the fox’s greatest discovery:

You don’t have to take everything you can. Sometimes leaving something behind makes you richer.

Abundance isn’t merely having more.

It’s learning how to make more possible. ;)